Q1. What is Exception Handling in PL/SQL?

Interview Ready Answer

Exception Handling is a mechanism in PL/SQL used to handle runtime
errors that occur during program execution.

Instead of terminating the program abnormally, it allows the program
to handle the error gracefully.

Key Points

• Handles runtime errors.
• Improves program reliability.
• Prevents abnormal termination of PL/SQL block.
• Executed through the EXCEPTION section.

======================================================================

Q2. Why do we need Exception Handling?

Interview Ready Answer

Exception Handling is required to handle unexpected runtime errors
without terminating the PL/SQL block abruptly.

Advantages

• Prevents abnormal program termination.
• Displays meaningful error messages.
• Improves application reliability.
• Allows alternative actions after an error.

Real-Life Example

Bank Withdrawal

If withdrawal amount > available balance

↓

Raise Exception

↓

Display

"Insufficient Balance"

Instead of terminating the transaction.

======================================================================

Q3. Difference between Compile-Time Error and Runtime Error.

Interview Ready Answer

Compile-Time Error

• Occurs during compilation.
• Caused by syntax or semantic mistakes.
• Program cannot execute until fixed.

Examples

• Missing semicolon.
• Misspelled keyword.
• Invalid syntax.

------------------------------------------------------------

Runtime Error

• Occurs during execution.
• Program compiles successfully.
• Can be handled using EXCEPTION block.

Examples

• ZERO_DIVIDE
• NO_DATA_FOUND
• TOO_MANY_ROWS
• VALUE_ERROR

======================================================================

Q4. What are the Types of Exceptions in PL/SQL?

Interview Ready Answer

PL/SQL provides two types of exceptions.

1. Predefined Exceptions
2. User-Defined Exceptions

======================================================================

Q5. What are Predefined Exceptions?

Interview Ready Answer

Predefined exceptions are built-in exceptions provided by Oracle.

They are automatically raised by the PL/SQL runtime system whenever
a specific runtime error occurs.

Examples

• ZERO_DIVIDE
• NO_DATA_FOUND
• TOO_MANY_ROWS
• VALUE_ERROR
• INVALID_NUMBER

======================================================================

Q6. What are User-Defined Exceptions?

Interview Ready Answer

User-defined exceptions are custom exceptions created by the
programmer to handle application-specific business rules.

They are explicitly raised using the RAISE statement.

Examples

• Age must be greater than 18.
• Salary cannot be negative.
• Account balance below minimum balance.

======================================================================

Q7. Difference between Predefined Exception and User-Defined Exception.

Interview Ready Answer

Predefined Exception

• Provided by Oracle.
• Automatically raised.
• Handles standard runtime errors.

Examples

• ZERO_DIVIDE
• NO_DATA_FOUND

------------------------------------------------------------

User-Defined Exception

• Created by programmer.
• Raised using RAISE statement.
• Handles business-specific rules.

Examples

• Invalid Age.
• Invalid Salary.
• Duplicate Registration.

======================================================================

Q8. What is the Purpose of EXCEPTION Block?

Interview Ready Answer

The EXCEPTION block is used to handle runtime errors that occur
during execution of the BEGIN section.

When an exception occurs, control is transferred to the matching
exception handler.

Purpose

• Handle runtime errors.
• Display meaningful messages.
• Execute alternative logic.
• Prevent abnormal termination.

======================================================================

Q9. What happens if there is no EXCEPTION Block?

Interview Ready Answer

If a runtime exception occurs and no matching EXCEPTION handler
exists, the PL/SQL block terminates abnormally and Oracle returns
the error to the calling program or application.

Important

The PL/SQL block terminates.

The Oracle Database does NOT crash.

======================================================================

Q10. What is the RAISE Statement?

Interview Ready Answer

RAISE is a PL/SQL statement used to explicitly raise a user-defined
exception.

Purpose

• Trigger custom exceptions.
• Implement business rules.
• Transfer control to EXCEPTION block.

